tation","color":"ffe7a8","default":true,"description":""},{"id":1143569068,"node_id":"MDU6TGFiZWwxMTQzNTY5MDY4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/labels/datatypes","name":"datatypes","color":"59d67b","default":false,"description":"things to do with database types, like VARCHAR and others"}],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignee":null,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":11,"created_at":"2019-03-28T04:45:01Z","updated_at":"2019-03-29T16:53:36Z","closed_at":"2019-03-29T13:47:16Z","author_association":"NONE","type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"Hi guys,\r\n\r\nStarting on 1.3.0 (also tested on 1.3.1), SQLAlchemy ORM suddenly stopped casting fields defined with ARRAY of Enum values. This works fine on 1.2.18, but as soon as I update to the next version it breaks, leading to a situation where I can't add an instance to a session by setting this kind of field to a list of enum values.\r\n\r\nI'm not sure if this is a bug or if the library was changed in a way that this behavior is intended, but is anybody else aware of this?\r\n\r\nPlease let me know if you want me to provide more details, or a test case perhaps for regression testing - I just wanted to be sure if this is really a bug and not an expected change in behavior first.\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\nDiogo","closed_by":{"login":"sqlalchemy-bot","id":36047385,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2MDQ3Mzg1","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/36047385?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot","html_url":"https://github.com/sqlalchemy-bot","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https